%S A400143 1,2,2,3,2,4,2,4,2,4,2,6,2,4,4,5,2,4,2,6,4,4,2,8,2,4,4,6,2,8,2,6,4,4,
%T A400143 4,6,2,4,4,8,2,8,2,6,4,4,2,10,2,4,4,6,2,8,4,8,4,4,2,12,2,4,4,7,4,8,2,
%U A400143 6,4,8,2,8,2,4,4,6,4,8,2,10,2,4,2,12,4,4,4,8,2,8
%N A400143 The number of divisors d of n such that gcid(d, n/d) is a power of 2, where gcid is the greatest common infinitary divisor.
%C A400143 First differs from A399833 at n = 81.
%C A400143 The sum of these divisors is A400146(n).
%H A400143 Amiram Eldar, <a href="/A400143/b400143.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%F A400143 Multiplicative with a(2^e) = e+1, and a(p^e) = 2^A000120(e) = A001316(e) for an odd prime p.
%F A400143 a(n) = A000005(n) * A037445(n) / A400142(n).
%F A400143 Dirichlet g.f.: (1/(1 - 1/2^s)^2) * Product_{p odd prime, k>=0} (1 + 2/p^(2^k*s)).
%t A400143 f[2, e_] := e+1; f[p_, e_] := 2^DigitSum[e, 2];
%t A400143 a[1] = 1; a[n_] := Times @@ f @@@ FactorInteger[n]; Array[a, 100]
%o A400143 (PARI) a(n) = {my(e = valuation(n, 2)); (e+1) * vecprod(apply(x -> 2^hammingweight(x), factor(n >> e)[, 2]));}
%Y A400143 Cf. A000005, A000120, A001316, A365207 (analogous with gcd), A399833 (unitary analog), A400137, A400146.
%Y A400143 The number of divisors d of n such that gcid(d, n/d) is: A037445 (1), A400138 (squarefree), A400139 (powerful), A400140 (square), A400141 (exponentially odd), A400142 (odd), this sequence (power of 2).
%K A400143 nonn,mult,easy,new
%O A400143 1,2
%A A400143 _Amiram Eldar_, Sep 20 2026
